Decoder: units in a JIT engine's code pool stop where code is already translated

A unit (multiblock) runs on from its entry through every branch target and call return in range, up to
MaxInstPerBlock (5,000). In JavaScriptCore's code pool every call's return address is an entry point and inline
cache stubs jump back into the middle of functions, so FEX compiled a new unit at each of those addresses and each
one decoded on to the end of the function again. With the A380X's SystemsHost gauge (Bun 1.4.4, JIT on, cold, 300
frames) FEX translated 68-78 million guest instructions from the pool for about 1.2 million that run; 97 % of them
lay at or past an address that was already a live entry point of another unit. The big units spanned many pages,
so each write fault (a new function, an inline cache repatch) threw away more of them, 70 % of the pool's compiles
were entries compiled before, and the 128 MB code buffer filled and was cleared 10 times. The first 300 frames took
25-30 s with frames of 6 s; in Microsoft Flight Simulator this is Coherent's UI Thread when the cockpit loads
(squark records 25.83).

When the unit's entry is in a JIT engine's code pool (0041's test, a private executable reservation of 256 MB or
more), the decoder stops where this thread's code buffer already holds a live entry point:

- an instruction reached by falling through ends the block before it (FinishOp then exits to it, linkable);
- a branch target there is not added to the unit (the branch exits to it);
- a call's return address there is not added either. The call still pushes a call-ret entry, for a linked branch
  to that address placed right after the call (JIT ExitFunction, SquarkJoinedReturn), so the return stays
  predicted by the call-ret stack and the host's return stack. That branch runs none of this unit's guest code:
  it stays correct when this unit is erased, and the other unit's own exits and 0040's stale exits cover it being
  erased.

The pool test runs once per compile and only when a live entry is met, so units elsewhere are not affected.
SQUARK_FEX_JITPOOL_JOIN=0 decodes as before; =2 stops at live entries in any writable code (measuring only).
SQUARK_FEX_HOT_JITPOOLS=1 no longer turns the pool test off for this (SqJitPoolTestFn, set unconditionally).

SystemsHost cold, 300 frames: 1.64 / 1.68 / 1.65 s (was 24.5-29.6 s), longest frame 332-365 ms (6.1-6.9 s), pool
instructions translated 1.33 M (78.3 M with the switch off in the same build), code buffer clears 2 (10).

